
  bar -- BAshRulez the bash rule evaluator

ABOUT

  Bar is a command runner that determines the order of commands and which ones to run, based
  on rules. It lets the user define rules that dependently and conditionally evaluate shell
  statements.  This is similar to other tools such as 'make' or 'just' but adds some
  opinionated differences. There is support for using bar as driver for githooks for
  automating workflows.

  The notable differences to other similar tools are:

  * Rules in bar can have multiple clauses.
  * Normally rules are conjunctive, when a clause of a rule fails then the rule fails and not
    further evaluated.
  * Rules can be made disjunctive, then a rule succeeds on the first succeeding clause.
  * Clauses can be conditionally skipped.
  * The results of rules are cached. Normally rules are evaluated only once, although there is
    a form to define rules that always evaluate.
  * We have a module that can memorize commands persistently and schedule evaluation to the
    background. A later instance can pick up the results from this background evaluation.

  When you read this because you have seen 'bar' or '.bar' used in a repository then you may
  look at INITIAL INSTALLATION below.


QUICKSTART

  When you cloned a project that uses bar then you can:

   ./bar                # just runs whatever the maintainer decided as default
   ./bar watch          # watches the project for changes and runs the default
   ./bar watch fastchk  # watches the project for changes and runs fast checks
   ./bar activate       # installs githooks and other local prep work

  When you want to install and use it in your own projects:

   git clone https://seed.pipapo.org/z3WhBdHt1VVNyeQ61zpY66pNzuSrP.git bar
   cd bar
   ./bar init_install ~/.local/
   git pull   # to get updates

  Then you can initialize it in your projects with:

   bar init
   git add Barf Bar.d .gitignore
   git commit -m 'start using bar'

   bar init_update  # for updating the version stored in the project

  The second personality of bar is 'please' eg.:

   please wake <hostname>     # sends a wake-on-lan packet to a host
   please suspend <hostname>  # remotely sends a host to suspend-to-ram

  Buildin help system:

   bar help [topic]
   ./bar help [topic]


INVOCATION AND SEMANTICS

   bar [rulefile] [--bare] [rule [arguments..]]
   please [rulefile] [--bare] [rule [arguments..]]
   ./bar [rulefile] [--bare] [rule [arguments..]]
   <symlink> [arguments..]

  Starts by loading all initial modules (those which have underscores in their name) and the
  rulefile, which is either "Barf", "barf", ".Barf", ".barf" when called as 'bar' or "Pleasef"
  "pleasef" ".Pleasef" ".pleasef" "$HOME/.Pleasef" when called as 'please' and when the first
  argument is not a file.

  The duality of having 'bar' installed as 'please' is not only for making it more
  polite. This allows for a user to have private '.Pleasef' defining rules that are for common
  administration tasks distinct from versioned per-project 'Barf' and 'Bar.d' rules.

  Note that the rules for looking up the Barf/Pleasef and the modules are somewhat distinct.
  Nevertheless modules are in either way only loaded from a single directory and are not
  located in different paths. This is a opinionated choice to make the module system
  self-contained and not depend on secondary locations.

  That single directory is the closest rule directory ('Bar.d', 'bar.d', '.Bar.d' or '.bar.d')
  in the current directory or, walking upwards, in an ancestor of it.  The walk stops at the
  filesystem boundary unless '$BAR_CROSS_FS' is set.  When no rule directory exists, the
  modules installed below '$BAR_PREFIX' are used and '$BAR_TOPLEVEL' stays the directory bar
  was invoked from: it is the directory that holds the rule directory, and the rulefile lookup
  and everything else bar keeps in the project (the memodb state, run logs) resolves below it
  -- never below an ancestor or the filesystem root.

  Then the given rule (or MAIN) with the supplied arguments becomes evaluated. Finally, if
  a 'CLEANUP' rule exists, it is evaluated.

  The exit code of the invocation is the exit code of the main rule.

  Just calling 'bar' without any arguments will evaluate the 'MAIN' rule which should be what
  people expect by default. Other useful rules like 'help' for this documentation are defined
  by modules.

  When called by a symlink, the standard rulefile is loaded and instead of ‘MAIN’, a rule with
  the same name as the symlink is called with all arguments passed.


MODULE API/RULES

  agent_rules - The agent output contract -- a run reduced 'up to the point'
    
    bar is written for humans: a run prints everything every tool produced.  In a terminal that is
    right, in an agent's context window it is fatal -- a full 'bar tests' here is ~400 lines /
    16 kB of which the actionable part is a handful of lines.
    
    This module turns a *run* into a small, decision shaped *answer*.  Three levels of progressive
    disclosure, the caller picks the level:
    
      bar verdict <target>..         one line per target                      (polling, gating)
      bar check   <target>..         verdict + chain + totals + bounded, line numbered evidence + log path
      bar log     <target> [range]   the untruncated raw log, on demand
    
    The contract ('never lie') is:
    
      * the verdict line is printed always, for every target, at every verbosity -- never filtered,
        never truncated;
      * on failure the exact totals (lines and bytes) of the log are always printed, even when
        there is no evidence to show at all;
      * the raw log is written to disk before anything is printed and its absolute path is printed
        on failure.  It is kept for successes too, so 'bar log' always works;
      * only bar's own machine emitted records ('OK:' 'FAIL:' 'CHECK:' 'RUN:' 'EVAL:') are pattern
        matched.  Foreign tool text is *never* filtered by keyword -- doing exactly that is how the
        real shellcheck findings got lost once -- it is bounded *positionally* (a head and a tail
        window) and the elision is announced with the exact log line range that was left out;
      * every evidence line carries its line number in the log, so 'bar log <target> <n>' (or
        'sed -n <n>p <log>') retrieves that line again, byte for byte;
      * a failure is never masked: the exit code is the rc of the first failing target;
      * bar's own options are never run as targets either.  Everything after a rule name belongs to
        that rule, so 'bar check agent --quiet' hands the option to 'check' -- it is dropped (with a
        NOTE from verbosity 3 on) instead of being reported as the phantom result 'OK --quiet'.  A
        target whose *name* begins with a dash stays reachable behind a bare '--': 'bar check --
        --weird' runs the target '--weird'.
    
    Measured on bar itself: 'bar tests' 16 kB / 409 lines -> 'bar check tests' 9 B / 1 line
    (success), and a 401 line failure of 14,6 kB -> ~1 kB carrying the verdict, the failing chain,
    the totals and the line that holds the signal.
    
    Exit codes: 0 when every target passed, 2 on a usage error, otherwise the rc of the first
    failing target propagated verbatim (3, 128+signal, ...).  'bar log' answers 3 when no log is on
    record for the target.
    
    The log is a *raw* capture: when the invoking stderr was a terminal, bar's own record lines in
    it carry that terminal's escape sequences.  The printed evidence never does (it is normalized)
    and the totals always describe the file exactly as it is on disk.

    Variables:

      BAR_AGENT_DIR (gx)
        Directory for the run logs below $BAR_TOPLEVEL.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_DIR:-.bar.agent}

      BAR_AGENT_INNER (gx)
        Verbosity the measured run is forced to.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_INNER:-2}

      BAR_AGENT_HEAD (gx)
        Evidence lines taken from the head of the log.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_HEAD:-8}

      BAR_AGENT_TAIL (gx)
        Evidence lines taken from the tail of the log.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_TAIL:-4}

      BAR_AGENT_MAX_LINE (gx)
        Maximum bytes of a single evidence line.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_MAX_LINE:-384}

      BAR_AGENT_MAX_BYTES (gx)
        Maximum bytes of a printed failure block.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_MAX_BYTES:-4096}

      BAR_AGENT_KEEP (gx)
        Number of runs (log directories) to keep.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_KEEP:-5}

      BAR_AGENT_JSON (g)
        When set, print one JSON object per run.
        Default: ${BAR_AGENT_JSON:-}

    Functions:

      agent_run_dir - create this run's directory, prune old ones, update 'latest'
        The directory is named after the timestamp of this invocation and the tree it runs in, the
        same way memodb names its trees.  'latest' is a convenience symlink for humans, it is
        explicitly *not* a way to find a log (see agent_find_log).

      agent_tree_id - identifies the tree this run is made in

      agent_keep - BAR_AGENT_KEEP, clamped to at least one

      agent_gc - keep the newest BAR_AGENT_KEEP run directories, delete the rest
        Only directories that look like a run directory ('<timestamp>-<tree>') are candidates, and
        only below our own run directory, so 'latest' and anything else is never touched.  A failure
        to prune is not a failure of the run that happens to trigger the prune.

      agent_find_log <target>
        newest log for <target>, newest run first

      agent_strip_ansi <text>
        remove terminal control sequences from a single line
        For the few lines bar interprets itself; the bulk of the normalization happens in
        agent_evidence.

      agent_fail_chain <target> <log>
        the failing chain, target plus bar's own records
        bar print a ' FAIL: <rule>: <dependency>' record when a *dependency* fails, but a clause
        *body* that fails just returns -- a target failing on its own therefore leaves no record at
        all.  The chain always starts at the target and is extended by the records found in the log,
        capped at four of them (the chain is part of the block budget).

      agent_evidence <log> <total-lines> <budget-bytes>
        bounded excerpt as 'E'/'X' records
        Streams the log once and prints at most BAR_AGENT_HEAD + BAR_AGENT_TAIL evidence lines as
        'E <log-line-number> <text>' records, preceded by one 'X <from> <to>' record when lines are
        elided ('X 0 0' means there is no evidence at all).
        Evidence is *selected positionally*: the first and the last lines of the log that are
        neither bar's own records nor empty.  No keyword decides what an agent gets to see.  Lines
        longer than BAR_AGENT_MAX_LINE are clipped at a word boundary with the number of bytes left
        out appended in '<+NB>', so nothing is hidden.  The scan streams: memory and output stay
        bounded for arbitrarily large logs.

      agent_rc_text <rc>
        printable rc, '?' when it is not a number

      agent_run_target <target> <dir>
        run one target, write its merged log, print its rc
        The target runs in a subshell with stdout and stderr merged into one log, so the log keeps
        the order in which the tool wrote it (memodb keeps the two streams in separate files and
        loses that order).  The subshell also contains a target whose clause body calls 'exit' --
        that would otherwise take the whole report down with it -- and hands back its real rc.
        The verbosity of the inner run is pinned so the records bar writes into the log do not
        depend on how 'bar check' itself was called, and the output format of the outer invocation
        is dropped: a target that calls bar itself (a project checking its own checks) must not
        suddenly print JSON just because 'bar --json check <target>' was used.

      agent_max_block - bytes of BAR_AGENT_MAX_BYTES that may go into evidence
        The block carries a fixed part (verdict, chain, totals, log path) and the elision marker;
        both are reserved before the evidence budget is handed to agent_evidence.

      agent_excerpt_print <log> <total> <budget>
        render the excerpt of agent_evidence

      agent_report_failure <target> <log> <rc>
        print the bounded failure block

      agent_json_string <text>
        a JSON string literal

      agent_json_evidence <log> <total> <budget>
        the evidence and elision members

      agent_json_target <target> <log> <rc>
        one JSON object for a measured target
        The same fields the text block carries: rc, chain, the byte exact totals and the log path,
        plus the bounded evidence.  Consumers that need a parser get one; the terse text stays the
        default because an agent reads lines, not braces.

      agent_json_success <target> <log>
        one JSON object for a target that passed

      agent_run_block <mode> <target>..
        body of the 'check' and 'verdict' rules
        <mode> is 'verdict' (one line per target, plus the log path on failure) or 'check' (the
        full bounded failure block).  Nothing changes the verdict itself: that line is always
        printed, at every verbosity.
        Everything after the rule name belongs to the rule, so bar's own options land here when they
        are written *after* the target ('bar check agent --quiet').  Such an argument is not a
        target: running it would print a verdict line for it, and a consumer reads 'OK --quiet' as a
        measured result -- a lie shaped line in the one surface that must never lie.  Dashed
        arguments are therefore dropped (a NOTE from verbosity 3 on names them), and a bare '--'
        turns the dropping off again, so a target whose name starts with a dash stays reachable:
        'bar check -- --weird'.

      agent_log_body <target> [<from>,<to>|tail:<n>]
        print the raw log of a previous run

    Rules:

      check
        Run targets and print a bounded verdict block: one 'OK <target>' line when a target passed,
        otherwise its verdict, the failing chain, the exact totals of its raw output, a bounded and line
        numbered excerpt of it and the path of the untruncated log.  Exits with the rc of the first
        failing target.  Only targets are run: an argument that begins with a dash -- bar's own option
        written after the target, 'bar check agent --quiet' -- is not one and is dropped with a notice
        from verbosity 3 on.  Write the option before the rule ('bar --quiet check agent') or a target
        whose name begins with a dash behind a '--' ('bar check -- --weird').

      verdict
        Run targets and print one line per target only ('OK <target>' / 'FAIL <target> (rc=..)').  The
        cheapest form, meant for polling loops and gating conditions; a failure additionally gets the
        path of its log so it can be retrieved with 'bar log'.  The same argument handling as for
        'check': a dashed argument after the target is dropped, never reported as a target of its own,
        and a '--' makes everything after it a target again.

      log
        Print the raw log a previous 'bar check'/'bar verdict' produced for <target>: everything, a line
        range <from>,<to>, or 'tail:<n>'.  Byte identical to what 'sed -n'/'tail -n' print.

      --json
        Machinereadable output: emit one JSON object per run (targets, rc, totals, evidence) instead of
        the terse text.  For wrapper scripts, not for reading.

  cargo - Rust/cargo support

    Variables:

      CARGO_TOOLCHAIN (xg)
        The rust toolchain that is used for most operations.
        Default: ${CARGO_TOOLCHAIN:-}

    Functions:

      is_cargo_tool_installed [+toolchain] <tool> [args..]
        Checks if 'cargo <tool>' is installed
        When [args..] is not present then '--version' is used. Tools that do not handle
        '--version' need 'args..' to give some sensible result.

      is_cargo_toolchain_available <+toolchain>
        Check if a toolchain is available

      cargo_tool_complete List available cargo tools/subcommands for completion
        This function provides completion for cargo tool names (subcommands).
        It lists both built-in cargo commands and installed extensions.

      cargo_fix Run 'cargo fix'.
        Note that this will modify the source in place.

      cargo_build [buildargs..]
        Compile a local package and all of its dependencies

      cargo_test [testargs..]
        Execute all unit and integration tests

      cargo_check Run 'cargo check'. Testing whenever the source can be compiled.

      cargo_clippy_errors Tests with 'cargo clippy' for errors only

      cargo_clippy_strict Test with 'cargo clippy' for warnings and errors

      cargo_doc [docargs..]
        Build this package's and its dependencies' documentation

      cargo_update Run 'cargo update'

      cargo_has_unsafe_code Checks if the source use 'unsafe' code.
        This does only a coarse '-Funsafe-code' check, but does not depend on external tools.

      cargo_miri [miriargs..]
        Run tests under miri (memory sanitizer)

      cargo_bench Run 'cargo bench'

      cargo_publish Run 'cargo publish'

    Rules:

      cargo_lint
        Runs all linters

      cargo_toolchain <+toolchain>
        Clause local change of the toochain used by cargo.

      cargo_fmt_check
        Checks whenever the source code is well formatted.
        If available the '+nightly' toolchain is used.

      cargo_build_unit_tests
        Builds unit tests.

      cargo_test_units
        Runs unit tests.

      cargo_build_integration_tests
        Build integration tests

      cargo_test_integrations
        Run integration tests. This includes the doctests.

      cargo_fmt
        Run 'cargo fmt'. When available the '+nightly' toolchain is used.
        Note that this will modify the source in place.

      cargo_miri
        When there is unsafe code then run a test under miri supervision.
        Requires the '+nightly' toolchain.

      cargo_msrv_check
        Checks whenever the 'rust_version' in the manifest is sufficient.
        This check only happens when 'cargo-msrv' is installed, otherwise a warning is printed.

      cargo_mutants
        Run 'cargo mutants' when it is available.
        When 'cargo-mutants' is not installed a warning is printed.

      cargo_outdated
        Run 'cargo outedated' when it is available.
        When 'cargo-outdated' is not installed a warning is printed.

      cargo_audit
        Run 'cargo audit' when it is available.
        When 'cargo-audit' is not installed a warning is printed.

      cargo_semver_checks
        Run cargo semver-checks to test if a semver for a release is sufficient

  cargo_rules - General support rules for Rust/Cargo projects
    
    Checks whenever we are in a cargo project.
    Hooks cargo support into the 'std_rules'

    Functions:

      has_cargo_manifest Checks if 'Cargo.toml' exists.

      has_cargo_lock Checks if 'Cargo.lock' exists.

    Rules:

      is_cargo_installed
        Successful when 'cargo' is available.

      is_cargo_project
        Check whenever we are ready to use cargo here.

      release_publish
        Push a software release to crates.io (or whatever registry is configured).

  cliff - git cliff changelog generation

    Variables:

      GIT_CLIFF_OUTPUT (gx)
        Output where to write the changelog
